LINE B — THE CONSENT BINDING

5. Read confirm/approvals.go in full. Create/Find both key on
   (tool, subject, opID) with all three required to match (approvals.go:91,
   :138). opID at all four call sites is the confirm token (verified by
   reading anchor.go, resolve.go, restore.go, shutdown.go end to end — one
   `token :=` declaration per handler, the same identifier passed to Check,
   askForApproval, awaitApproval and Redeem with no shadowing).

6. Token uniqueness: confirm.Registry.Mint (confirm.go:72) generates a fresh
   32-byte random token on every call regardless of whether the arguments are
   identical to a prior mint, so two concurrent set_balance_anchor previews
   for the same account (different amounts, or even the same amount) always
   get distinct opIDs — Create for the second never collides with or replaces
   the first's Pending. Ran the oracle's own concurrency-shaped checks
   (TestZZOracleR4_SameSubjectDifferentOperationsCoexist,
   _DecidingOneDoesNotAuthorizeTheOther, _OppositeVerdictsAreDistinctOperations)
   as part of accept.sh step 5 — all pass, and directly assert that deciding
   one pending does not release Await on the other.

7. Replayed-token-with-different-args attack: confirm.Check/Redeem hash the
   CURRENT call's arguments and compare against the hash stored at Mint time
   (confirm.go:102-146); a token minted for amount=500.00 presented against a
   call with amount=5000.00 fails hashArgs comparison and returns ErrBadToken
   before askForApproval/Create is ever reached (anchor.go:122-124,
   resolve.go:125-127 gate on Check first). This is pre-existing behaviour,
   not new to R4, and is exercised by the existing
   TestSetBalanceAnchorRefusesATokenForDifferentArguments and
   TestResolveTransferRefusesATokenForDifferentVerdict, both still present and
   passing.

8. Empty/defaulted opID: shutdown_server passes subject="" (as before) but
   opID=token (a real, unique, non-empty value at the only point opID is ever
   used — token is checked non-empty before reaching that code). The oracle's
   TestZZOracleR4_WrongOperationIDDoesNotMatch explicitly asserts that
   Find(tool, subject, "") does NOT match a bound approval, so an empty opID
   cannot silently degrade back to (tool, subject) matching even if some
   future caller passed one.

9. Expiry/sweep: Approvals.Find, Get and Create all call sweepLocked() before
   acting (approvals.go:88, :116, :136), and sweepLocked drops anything whose
   Expires has passed. A swept pending cannot be returned by Find. Covered by
   the pre-existing TestExpiredApprovalsAreSweptAway, unchanged and passing.

10. End-to-end browser round trip: internal/services/mcpsvc/admin/approval_test.go
    drives the FULL flow through a real in-memory MCP client/server session
    for restore_backup and shutdown_server (TestRestoreRunsWhenApprovedInTheBrowser,
    TestRestoreDoesNotRunWhenDeclinedInTheBrowser, TestRestoreDoesNotRunWhenNobodyAnswers,
    TestShutdownRunsWhenApprovedInTheBrowser, TestShutdownDoesNotRunWhenDeclinedInTheBrowser,
    TestWithoutBrowserWiringTheToolsFallBack) — all PASS under -race, run by me.
    Human-facing detail text: anchorConsequences and resolveConsequences are
    unchanged from HEAD (diffed against `git show 1a8636d`) and still name the
    concrete amount/date/verdict, so what the human reads on the approval page
    is genuinely the operation being authorized.

